{"kind":"artifact","text":"Deployment note repaired at exact head df27e17c51d35bf4297584923aef88a767c8102d, addressing all four findings in aea5d9b993d6164bd10e31d02d16aafd3c3b25fd. Two of them were consequences of my previous repair rather than of the original design, which is the more useful lesson and the reason I asked you to check for overcorrection. F1: the digest input was called \"an unambiguous encoding\" and left there — the same gap as \"compare-and-swap push\" in the claim step, naming a property instead of specifying it, twice in one note. It is now byte-exact: uint64be length-prefixed fields concatenated in fixed order, which is injective, so no shift of bytes between adjacent fields yields the same input; concatenation would collide (\"ab\",\"c\") with (\"a\",\"bc\"), and a delimiter scheme fails the moment a key contains the delimiter. The key is hashed as the exact octets the submitter sent with no normalisation, deliberately, so the drain never hashes something the submitter did not write and the submitter owns its own byte sequence. Case 9 now requires boundary-shift tuples and an independently written producer and drain, since a digest computed twice by the same code proves nothing. F2: dropping the push trigger invalidated the latency claim elsewhere in the note. The posture is now zero to five minutes plus unbounded delay, with the five-minute floor, load delay, dropped runs and default-branch-only execution stated; the claim-and-recover protocol already tolerates a dropped tick because nothing expires on a timer. Public-repository auto-disable after 60 inactive days is now in the runbook rather than absent, since a quiet workroom would otherwise stop draining silently, and the submitter-facing contract is the three retained terminal states rather than a time bound. F3: the same trigger change made case 2 vacuous — with no push trigger, N branches and one scheduled run trivially satisfy \"at most N runs\", so it proved backlog draining while claiming to prove coalescing. Split into 2a backlog drain and 2b coalescing, where 2b deliberately overlaps a scheduled tick with a manual trusted run. F4: case 6 promised the next run would re-push the true head, which an ephemeral runner reading only the vandalised copy cannot do, so the availability posture rested on a recovery step that did not exist. The control repository now holds a fast-forward-only refs/seq-mirror advanced in the same run that publishes; a run whose head is not a descendant of the mirror refuses and raises rather than recovering onto a history it cannot justify; recovery is a deliberate operator act by a party holding a copy; and absent a mirror the honest claim is detection, which the posture now says. The spike list ends with the standing hazard, since two of its cases could pass without exercising their subject: the test is always whether a case would fail if its subject were removed. Gates green: vet, test, git diff --check, worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"df27e17c51d35bf4297584923aef88a767c8102d","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}